    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Shanghai began as a fishing village and a market town on the Huangpu River, rose with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> trade and the Qing customs office, and became one of five treaty ports after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> ended in 1842.</li><li>From 1843 foreign concessions grew beside the walled Chinese city, and in the 1920s and 1930s the city was a commercial and financial hub of the Asia-Pacific region, the birthplace of Chinese cinema and the place where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> was founded.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded in 1932 and took the Chinese-held districts in 1937, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> took the city in 1949, and economic reform from 1990 brought the development of Pudong.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first Western atelier in Shanghai was established in 1864 inside the Tushanwan orphanage, and the city produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first fictional feature film, An Orphan Rescues His Grandfather, in 1923.</p></aside>
    </section>

