    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">746</span><span class="rf-text">Qinglong Town is established and becomes a major trading port</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1292</span><span class="rf-text">Shanghai becomes a county under Songjiang Prefecture</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">16th century</span><span class="rf-text">A city wall is built to protect the town from Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1842</span><span class="rf-text">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> opens Shanghai as one of five treaty ports</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1921</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> is founded in the French Concession</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1937</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shanghai">Battle of Shanghai</a> ends in Japanese occupation of the Chinese-held districts</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> takes control of the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1990</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> permits economic reforms and Pudong is developed</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shanghai stands on the southern estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a>, on an alluvial plain formed by the river's deposits and by later land reclamation. The south flank of the Yangtze Delta began to develop after the maximum Holocene marine transgression, and as sediment built up new land, shell-rich sand ridges known as Gangshen formed in what are now the Jinshan and Fengxian districts. The earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements known in the area date to the Majiabang culture (5000 to 3300 BCE), overlapped by the Songze culture from about 3800 to 3300 BCE. At the Songze site in Qingpu District archaeologists found the prone skeleton of a man aged 25 to 30, one of the earliest known inhabitants. By the 4th century the shoreline had reached the Xinzhuang, Minhang and Nanqiao line, and the land east of it was fully formed before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a> the area belonged to the Kingdom of Wu, which Yue conquered and Chu in turn conquered. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> it lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-chunshen">Lord Chunshen</a> of Chu, and local legends say he ordered the Huangpu River dug, which gave the city the nickname Shen. Fishermen made a fish tool called the hu, which named the outlet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> and became the city's abbreviation. In 751 Huating County was established as the first county-level administration within modern Shanghai. The name Shanghai, written with the characters for 'upon' and 'sea', first appears in the 11th century, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Shanghai&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology, Alternative names:</p><ul><li>Li Choy Chong, Business Environment and Opportunities in China: Shanghai and its Surrounding Region (Deutscher Universitätsverlag, 1998)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor.jpg" alt="Kangxi Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B8%85%E5%9C%A3%E7%A5%96%E5%BA%B7%E7%86%99%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author Qing Dynasty Court Painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first great port of the area was not Shanghai but Qinglong Town in modern Qingpu District. Established in 746, it traded along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the Chinese coast and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and Silla, and was later called a giant town of the Southeast. By 1077 the commercial tax collected there exceeded all the other taxes of the county combined. Siltation then narrowed the Wusong River and Qinglong declined, and the outer port of Huating County moved east to Shanghai Town on the developing Huangpu. Shanghai was raised from a village to a market town in 1074. Around the turn of the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> growing and textile techniques, traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-daopo">Huang Daopo</a>, spread from Shanghai across the Yangtze Delta, and under the Ming cotton textiles were rapidly commercialised.</p><p>Two Qing policy changes made Shanghai one of the most important seaports of the delta. In 1684 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> reversed the prohibition on oceangoing vessels of 1525, and in 1732 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> moved the customs office for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a> province from Songjiang to Shanghai. By 1735 it was the major trade port of the lower Yangtze, although it stood at the lowest level of the administrative hierarchy. By the mid-18th century wharves, warehouses, the customs house and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> halls had spread beyond the city walls. In the 1920s and 1930s the city was a commercial and financial hub of the Asia-Pacific region, and in 1936 it was one of the largest cities in the world, with 3 million inhabitants and about half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s imports and exports.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The area belonged successively to the kingdoms of Wu, Yue and Chu and was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-chunshen">Lord Chunshen</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. Huating County was established in 751 by Zhao Juzhen, governor of Wu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a>, and was governed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> and later Jiaxing. In 1277 Huating was raised to Songjiang Prefecture, and in the early 1290s five townships of eastern Huating were separated to form Shanghai County, with its seat at Shanghai Town. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> until 1927 central Shanghai was administered as a county under Songjiang Prefecture.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> established a presence outside the walled city, which remained under direct Chinese administration. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai-municipal-council">Shanghai Municipal Council</a> was formed in 1854 to manage the foreign settlements, and in 1863 the British and American settlements joined as the Shanghai International Settlement, while the French kept their own concession. The council was wholly foreign-controlled, and no Chinese resident of the Settlement could join it until 1928. The Old City walls were pulled down in 1912.</p><p>On 7 July 1927 Shanghai was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a> and became a Chinese municipality under a mayor and council. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the Chinese-held districts after 1937 and the foreign concessions from 8 December 1941, and held the whole city until its surrender in 1945. The concessions ended in 1943 for the British and Americans and in 1946 for the French. In 1949 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> took control, and Shanghai was one of three municipalities not merged into neighbouring provinces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-bund"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-bund.jpg" alt="The Bund" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/the-bund">The Bund</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bund_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">钉钉, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the treaty system the city was divided between a more European western half and a more Chinese eastern half. Europeans and Americans called themselves Shanghailanders, and the Public Garden north of the Bund was for decades reserved for foreign nationals. The compradors, mediators for the foreign companies, formed a new class that mixed with local landlords into a Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>. Chinese society was organised in native place associations, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of traders from shared hometowns. White Russians who fled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in the 1920s and 1930s formed the second-largest foreign community, and about 30,000 Jewish refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> arrived in the 1930s.</p><p>Faiths and buildings reflected these arrivals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> had a presence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>, when the Longhua and Jing'an temples were founded. The City God Temple was built in 1602, an honour usually reserved for prefectural capitals. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-guangqi">Xu Guangqi</a> was baptised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a> in 1603, and the Italian missionary Lazzaro Cattaneo brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1608. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, with Songjiang Mosque. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bund">The Bund</a> took its early 20th-century form, and the city developed Art Deco buildings and shikumen houses of grey brick arranged in alleys called longtangs.</p><p>The vernacular is Shanghainese, a Northern Wu language. In the late 19th century it replaced Suzhounese as the prestige dialect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> Delta. Shanghai was also the birthplace of Chinese cinema, with the short film The Difficult Couple in 1913 and the first fictional feature in 1923.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Shanghai&#8221; cites where it covers Performance arts:</p><ul><li>Jonathan Stock, Huju: Traditional Opera in modern Shanghai (Oxford; New York: Published for the British Academy by Oxford University Press, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shanghai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-shanghai.jpg" alt="Battle of Shanghai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shanghai">Battle of Shanghai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shanghai1937KMT_machine_gun_nest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1853 the Chinese-held Old City fell to the Small Swords Society, rebels linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, and the Qing government regained it in February 1855. Between 1860 and 1862 Taiping forces twice attacked Shanghai and destroyed the eastern and southern suburbs but failed to take the city.</p><p>On 28 January 1932 Japanese forces invaded. More than 10,000 shops and hundreds of factories and public buildings were destroyed, Zhabei district was left in ruins, and about 18,000 civilians were killed, injured or declared missing. A ceasefire came on 5 May. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shanghai">Battle of Shanghai</a> in 1937 ended in the occupation of the Chinese-held districts. Under the occupation, people in the city experienced hunger, oppression or death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> placed Jewish refugees in the Shanghai Ghetto in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongkou">Hongkou</a>, where hunger and infectious diseases such as dysentery were common, and interned Britons, Americans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in camps, notably Lunghua, in 1943.</p><p>After the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> overwhelmed the city. In 1948 a price index rose from 2,199 in late November to 3,483 in early January 1949, then to 35,774 in early February and above 3,000,000 by April, and industrial output fell to 50 to 60 percent of its early 1948 level. Nationalist forces had vowed to make Shanghai a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>, but lost 153,000 of 210,000 defenders in the outer districts and did not hold the centre. In 1950 Nationalist air raids killed or injured many civilians, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> of 1966 to 1976 severely damaged the city's society.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping.jpg" alt="Deng Xiaoping" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deng_Xiaoping_at_the_arrival_ceremony_for_the_Vice_Premier_of_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shanghai's modern history is closely tied to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> was founded in the French Concession in July 1921, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/may-thirtieth-movement">May Thirtieth Movement</a> of 1925, which began with the killing of a worker in a Japanese-owned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill, led to strikes and nationwide protests. The Greater Shanghai Plan of the late 1920s laid out a new city centre in Jiangwan, outside the concessions.</p><p>The 1920s and 1930s left a row of neoclassical and Art Deco buildings on the Bund, among them the HSBC Building and Sassoon House, and Art Deco works by the architect László Hudec, who lived in the city from 1918 to 1947. The Old City kept traditional architecture such as the Yu Garden. Under the People's Republic the old racecourse was reclaimed in 1951 and its northern section became People's Park. In 1990 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> permitted the city to begin economic reforms, which brought back foreign capital and developed Pudong and its financial district, Lujiazui.</p><p>Shanghai is today a provincial-level municipality divided into 16 districts, and the most populous city in China. The municipality has 24,874,500 people, of whom about 59.5 percent are registered locally, and about 40 percent come from other regions of China.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/mo-li-hua">Mo Li Hua<span>1765</span></a><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers<span>1935</span></a><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red<span>1942</span></a><a href="/songs/aegukka">Aegukka (North Korea)<span>1947</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Shanghai grew from a fishing village and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> market on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> estuary into a treaty port after 1842, with foreign concessions beside the Chinese city. It was a commercial hub in the 1930s, was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and was transformed again from 1990.</em></p>

